\paragraph{A matter inequality from the Vlasov representation.} At a spacetime point choose an orthonormal frame, particle mass $m_0\geq0$ and future momentum $p^0=\sqrt{m_0^2+|p|^2}$. For a nonnegative integrable distribution $f(p)$ define $T^{ab}=\int f(p)p^ap^b\,dp/p^0$, with the usual exclusion of the zero momentum singularity in the massless case. For any null vector $k$,
\[
 T_{ab}k^ak^b=\int f(p)(p_ak^a)^2\,dp/p^0\geq0.
\]
This proves the null energy inequality directly whenever the integral is finite; it does not depend on dust or scalar-field analogies.

\paragraph{A conditional focusing estimate.} For a hypersurface-orthogonal null congruence in four dimensions with expansion $\theta$, Raychaudhuri's equation and this inequality would give $d\theta/d\lambda\leq-\theta^2/2$ along an affinely parameterized generator. If $\theta(0)<0$ and the inequality remains valid before a caustic, integration of $d(1/\theta)/d\lambda\geq1/2$ forces blowup or breakdown by affine time at most $2/|\theta(0)|$. This is a local focusing consequence; the Raychaudhuri identity is stated as an explicit geometric input, not independently rederived.

\paragraph{Why censorship does not follow.} Focusing alone says nothing about whether the focusing region is hidden from complete future null infinity. The first missing step is global control of the Einstein--Vlasov development and its asymptotic outgoing geometry. Genericity, data topology and exceptional sets also remain unspecified in the broad source statement. The calculation neither transfers a scalar-field theorem nor proves the assertion for every spherically symmetric datum.

